Key Vault was designed to hand out secrets at deployment time, and its service limits aren't built for a read per request. When a caller exceeds them it gets HTTP 429. Learn's guidelines include:
• honour exponential back-off on 429s, and avoid immediate retries;
• split traffic across vaults, one per security or availability domain, such as app and region;
• cache secrets in memory and re-read only when the cached copy stops working;
• where the target service supports Microsoft Entra authentication, use it instead of a stored secret.
A subscription-wide limit for all transaction types is five times the limit of one vault.

Rotation is event-driven. Key Vault publishes events to Event Grid: SecretNewVersionCreated, SecretNearExpiry (30 days before the current version's expiry date) and SecretExpired, with matching events for keys and certificates. In Learn's single-credential tutorial, an Event Grid-triggered function generates a new password, stores it as a new secret version and updates the database server, and Event Grid retries a failed step for two hours. For services with two keys, such as storage accounts, the two-credential tutorial alternates the keys, so one valid key always exists. Learn's first choice is still a managed identity, with no secret to rotate.

A forecasting service reads its third-party map API password from an environment variable baked into its deployment settings. The map provider offers no Microsoft Entra sign-in, so a secret is unavoidable. The team:
1. creates one vault for this service in production, using the Azure RBAC model;
2. gives the service's managed identity Key Vault Secrets User at vault scope, and the platform engineers Key Vault Secrets Officer;
3. writes the password with set_secret and a content type that says what it is;
4. replaces the environment variable with a SecretClient call at start-up that reads the latest version and keeps it in memory for the life of the process.
The value no longer sits in deployment settings, each identity gets only the role it needs, and a later rotation only adds a new version, which the service picks up at its next start.

A forecasting API runs in two regions and reads its settings from one App Configuration store. During a short network fault in its home region, new instances failed to start because the store couldn't be reached. The team:
1. adds a geo-replica of the store in the second region; the Python provider discovers replicas by itself, so the load() call needs no new endpoint;
2. raises startup_timeout from the 100-second default to 300 seconds, so a cold start rides out a brief outage while load() retries;
3. keeps replica discovery switched on, which is the default.
New instances can now fall back to the replica when the home store can't be reached.

A scoring worker logs one line per batch, such as "Scored batch 1182 in 840 ms", and the team wants the slowest batch per hour for the last day. The numbers sit inside the message text, so the query:
1. starts from AppTraces with a TimeGenerated filter for the last day;
2. keeps the relevant lines with where Message has "Scored", a whole-term match that can use the index;
3. uses parse to pull Batch and Ms out as long columns in one pass, instead of several extract() calls;
4. ends with summarize slowest = max(Ms), batches = count() by bin(TimeGenerated, 1h).
Lines that don't follow the pattern get null in the parsed columns, so they don't distort the maximum.
